{{Use New Zealand English|date=November 2024}}

'''Charter schools in New Zealand''', also known as '''partnership schools''' or '''kura hourua''' in [[Māori language|te reo Māori]], are schools that receive government funding similar to state schools but are subject to fewer rules and regulations from the Ministry of Education.<ref name=nh-2011/> They are free and open for any students to attend. [[Charter schools]] had the autonomy to set their own curriculum, qualifications, pay rates for teachers, school-hours and school terms. The schools are operated by sponsors such as Māori [[iwi]], not-for-profit organisations, businesses or existing education providers.<ref name=nh-2011/>

Charter schools were legalised after an agreement between the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]] and their confidence and supply partner [[ACT New Zealand]] following the [[2011 New Zealand general election|2011 general election]]. In October 2012, the Education Amendment Bill creating charter schools passed with a five-vote majority.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |title=Education Amendment Bill - New Zealand Parliament |url=https://www.parliament.nz/en/pb/bills-and-laws/bills-proposed-laws/document/00DBHOH_BILL11822_1/education-amendment-bill |access-date=2022-11-16 |website=www.parliament.nz |language=en}}</ref> The charter school model was heavily criticized by a wide range of educational authorities, teacher organizations, the general public and political parties who vowed to overturn it.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Stuart|first1=Lynda|title=Charter schools: the case against|url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/society/18-05-2017/charter-schools-the-case-against/|accessdate=8 March 2018|publisher=[[The Spinoff]]|date=18 May 2017}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Charter schools: an update|url=http://ppta.org.nz/dmsdocument/207|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170408105640/http://ppta.org.nz/dmsdocument/207|url-status=usurped|archive-date=8 April 2017|publisher=[[Post Primary Teachers' Association]]|accessdate=8 March 2018}}</ref><ref name="Interest Election 2017">{{cite news|last1=Wiener|first1=Julia|title=Election 2017 – Party Policies – Education – Charter Schools|url=https://www.interest.co.nz/news/87419/election-2017-party-policies-education-charter-schools|accessdate=8 March 2018|publisher=Interest.co.nz|date=27 March 2017}}</ref> Opposition to charter schools formed part of the Labour Party's education policy in the 2014 and 2017 general elections.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Macartney |first=Bernadette |date=September 2014 |title=Opinion piece on Labour's Education Policy 2014 and progress towards an inclusive education system |url=https://ieag.org.nz/assets/Uploads/B_Macartney_Opinion_piece_Labours_2014_Education_Policy.pdf |access-date=16 November 2022}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Moir |first=Jo |date=2017-07-21 |title=Labour promises to scrap national standards and review NCEA if in government |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/education/94965404/labour-promises-to-scrap-national-standards-and-review-ncea-if-in-government |access-date=2022-11-16 |website=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |language=en}}</ref>

In late October 2017, the newly formed [[Sixth Labour Government of New Zealand|Labour-led coalition government]] announced that it would be abolishing charter schools.<ref name="NZ Herald Oct 2017">{{cite news|last1=Collins|first1=Simon|title=Labour's education plans revealed: Primary school league tables axed, big NCEA shakeup|url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=11937975|accessdate=1 November 2017|work=[[The New Zealand Herald]]|date=30 October 2017}}</ref> This announcement triggered mixed responses. Teaching unions supported the new Government's decision, while charter schools and the opposition National and ACT parties opposed the announcement.<ref name="PPTA March 2018">{{cite web|last1=Robinson|first1=Liz|title=Charter school removal a great day for students, teachers and parents|url=http://ppta.org.nz/news-and-media/charter-school-removal-a-great-day-for-students-teachers-and-parents/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180211091910/http://ppta.org.nz/news-and-media/charter-school-removal-a-great-day-for-students-teachers-and-parents|url-status=usurped|archive-date=11 February 2018|publisher=Post Primary Teachers' Association|accessdate=8 March 2018}}</ref><ref name="Herald 7 March 2018">{{cite news|last1=Collins|first1=Simon|title=Charter schools at odds over Chris Hipkins' plans to abolish them|url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=12006267|accessdate=8 March 2018|work=[[The New Zealand Herald]]|date=7 March 2018}}</ref><ref name="Nikki Kaye Stuff">{{cite news|last1=Walters|first1=Laura|title=National complains to Auditor-General about Government's handling of partnership school issue|url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/101608899/national-complains-to-auditorgeneral-about-governments-handling-of-partnership-school-issue|accessdate=8 March 2018|agency=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]]|date=20 February 2018}}</ref> In response to protests, Prime Minister [[Jacinda Ardern]] said that charter schools could convert to "special character" schools.<ref name="NZ Herald 8 March 2018">{{cite news|last1=Trevett|first1=Claire|title=PM says compromise will help charter schools to convert rather than close|url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=11992994|accessdate=8 March 2018|work=[[The New Zealand Herald]]|date=12 February 2018}}</ref><ref name="RNZ 14 Feb 2018">{{cite news|last1=Boynton|first1=John|title=Māori charter schools safe – Labour's Māori MPs|url=https://www.radionz.co.nz/news/te-manu-korihi/350396/maori-charter-schools-safe-labour-s-maori-mps|accessdate=8 March 2018|publisher=[[Radio New Zealand]]|date=14 February 2018}}</ref> By September 2018, all twelve remaining charter schools had successfully transitioned to become [[state-integrated school]]s.<ref name="Newshub Sep 2018">{{cite news |last1=Bracewell-Worrall |first1=Anna |title=All NZ charter schools now approved to become state integrated |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2018/09/all-nz-charter-schools-now-approved-to-become-state-integrated.html |access-date=20 March 2019 |publisher=[[Newshub]] |date=17 September 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181207143557/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2018/09/all-nz-charter-schools-now-approved-to-become-state-integrated.html |archive-date=7 December 2018 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

On November 24, 2023, the newly formed [[Sixth National Government of New Zealand|National/ACT/NZ First coalition government]] made a pledge to reinstate charter schools in New Zealand, marking a shift in education policy direction.<ref name="NZH coalition agreement">{{cite AV media |date=23 November 2023|title=National, Act and NZ First leaders sign the coalition agreement |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=i-uvdhMsj3w |format=YouTube video |publisher=[[The New Zealand Herald]]|access-date=24 November 2023}}</ref><ref name=newshub-election>{{cite news|last=Quinlivan |first=Mark|title= Election 2023: National, ACT and NZ First's Coalition agreement |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2023/11/election-2023-national-act-and-nz-first-s-coalition-agreement.html |access-date=27 November 2023|work=[[Newshub]] |date=24 November 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231124040207/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2023/11/election-2023-national-act-and-nz-first-s-coalition-agreement.html|archive-date=24 November 2023|url-status=dead}}</ref> On 25 September 2024, the [[New Zealand Parliament]] passed legislation reestablishing charter schools.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Smith |first1=Anneke |title=Charter schools revived as bill passes third reading |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/528977/charter-schools-revived-as-bill-passes-final-reading |access-date=25 September 2024 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=25 September 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240925031951/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/528977/charter-schools-revived-as-bill-passes-final-reading |archive-date=25 September 2024|url-status=live}}</ref>

==Context==
In 1989, the [[Fourth Labour Government of New Zealand|Fourth Labour Government]] reformed the state (public) school system in what was known as the "[[Tomorrow's Schools]]" reforms. Blaming the amount of centralised bureaucracy for slipping school standards, the government disestablished the Department of Education, replacing it with the smaller [[Ministry of Education (New Zealand)|Ministry of Education]] and moving the governance of state schools to their individual school communities.<ref>{{cite report |first= Mark |last= Adams |date= January 2009 |title= Tomorrow's schools Today: New Zealand's Experiment 20 years on |url= http://mercatus.org/sites/default/files/publication/WP0901_GAP_New_Zealand_Reforms.pdf |publisher=Mercatus Center, [[George Mason University]] |accessdate= 23 July 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite book|last1=Butterworth|first1=Graham|last2=Butterwoth|first2=Susan|title=Reforming Education: The New Zealand Experience, 1984–1996|date=1998|publisher=Dunmore Press|location=[[Palmerston North]]|isbn=0864693346|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=V0HMAAAACAAJ|accessdate=9 March 2018}}</ref> Since the [[Education Act 1877]], the New Zealand public primary schooling system has been free and secular with provision for private religious schools. In 1935, secondary school education was made free by the [[First Labour Government of New Zealand|First Labour Government]].<ref>{{cite book|last1=King|first1=Michael|title=The Penguin History of New Zealand|date=2003|publisher=Penguin Group (NZ)|location=North Shore, Auckland|isbn=9780143018674|pages=233–234, 356|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=LKQP2sw_BVoC|accessdate=9 March 2018}}</ref> State schools are [[crown entity|crown entities]], meaning they are government-owned and retain the strong governmental and parliamentary oversight and control in common with other New Zealand crown entities. State schools are the most common in New Zealand.<ref name="NZ Schooling System">{{cite web|title=The school system|url=https://www.newzealandnow.govt.nz/living-in-nz/education/school-system#section--1|website=New Zealand Now|publisher=[[Immigration New Zealand]]|accessdate=9 March 2018}}</ref>

Apart from the numerous state schools there are several variations on the Tomorrow's Schools model, the largest of which is [[state-integrated schools]]. State-integrated schools are parochial schools or private schools that have been "integrated" into the state school system under the Private Schools Conditional Integration Act 1975,<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.legislation.co.nz/act/public/1975/0129/latest/contents.html |title=Private Schools Conditional Integration Act 1975 |access-date=4 August 2013 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20130223214652/http://www.legislation.co.nz/act/public/1975/0129/latest/contents.html |archive-date=23 February 2013 |url-status=dead }}</ref> usually because they have run into financial difficulty. These schools are run the same as state schools, but they are allowed to retain their special character. Proprietors of the school (e.g. the Catholic Church in the case of a Catholic school) own the integrated school facilities and sit on the school's board of trustees, but they do not receive government funds to maintain [[separation of church and state]], and subsequently require parents to pay "attendance dues" for upkeep of the facilities.<ref>{{cite book|last1=O'Donnell|first1=Susan|title=The Character and Culture of the Catholic School|date=2001|publisher=New Zealand Council for Education Research|location=Wellington|isbn=1877140988|pages=43–69|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=sFIUAgAACAAJ&q=the+character+and+culture+of+the+catholic+school|accessdate=9 March 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite book|last1=Sweetman|first1=Rory|title=A Fair and Just Solution?: A History of the Integration of Private Schools in New Zealand|date=2002|publisher=Dunmore Press in association with the Ministry of Culture and Heritage|location=Palmerston North|isbn=0864694148|pages=13–22|url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/lifestyle/news/article.cfm?c_id=6&objectid=3197124|accessdate=9 March 2018}}</ref><ref name="NZ Schooling System" /> [[Kura Kaupapa schools]] are state-run schools with heavy emphasis on learning [[Māori language]] and [[Māori culture|culture]] but having most of the features of normal state schools.<ref>{{cite web|last1=Calman|first1=Ross|title=Māori education – mātauranga – Kaupapa Māori education|url=https://teara.govt.nz/en/maori-education-matauranga/page-5|publisher=Te Ara – the Encyclopedia of New Zealand|accessdate=9 March 2018}}</ref><ref name="School types">{{cite web|title=Different types of secondary schools|url=https://parents.education.govt.nz/secondary-school/secondary-schooling-in-nz/different-types-of-secondary-schools/|publisher=[[Ministry of Education (New Zealand)|Ministry of Education]]|accessdate=9 March 2018}}</ref> In addition there are fee-paying [[Independent school|private schools]] which receive limited funding from the state. Most follow the national curriculum or internationally recognized variations.<ref name="NZ Schooling System" /><ref name="School types" />

== Legislative implementation ==
[[File:Min. of Ed.jpg|thumb|upright|Education minister [[Hekia Parata]] introduced the partnership schools legislation in 2012.]]
Following the [[2011 New Zealand general election|2011 general election]], the National Party, in return for [[confidence and supply]], announced it would pick up an [[ACT New Zealand|ACT party]] policy of setting up charter schools in southern Auckland and eastern Christchurch within three years. The government intended to set up charter schools as alternatives to state schools. [[Hekia Parata]], the new education minister, would be responsible for their introduction. John Banks, the ACT leader, was made an associate minister of education and given responsibility for leading the work related to the development of potential charter schools.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2012-05-20 |title=Schedule of Responsibilities Delegated to Associate Ministers {{!}} DPMC |url=http://www.dpmc.govt.nz/cabinet/ministers/delegated#portfolio-23 |access-date=2022-11-16 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120520092009/http://www.dpmc.govt.nz/cabinet/ministers/delegated#portfolio-23 |archive-date=20 May 2012 }}</ref>

The government's proposal was, like in other countries, charter schools would be operated by private businesses or organisations and would be directly accountable for performance to the organisations running them. The schools would receive state funding and private donations, but have the same freedoms as private schools in matters such as in setting the curriculum, length of the school year and teachers' pay. Teaching staff would also not have to be registered or even formally trained (although they would still require police clearance). Charter schools would still be subject to triennial external reviews by the [[Education Review Office (New Zealand)|Education Review Office]], and the government would still have the power to intervene if there are serious problems, either taking over from the sponsor for a period of time or sacking them altogether.<ref>{{cite news |first= Derek |last= Cheng |date= 6 December 2011 |title= What are charter schools? |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |location= Auckland |url= http://www.nzherald.co.nz/election-2011/news/article.cfm?c_id=1503012&objectid=10771267 |accessdate= 21 January 2012}}</ref> In the original proposal, unlike state schools, charter schools would not be subject to either [[Office of the Ombudsman (New Zealand)|Ombudsman]] scrutiny or the [[Official Information Act 1982]].

The plan was heavily criticised by the opposition [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour]] and [[Green Party of Aotearoa New Zealand|Green]] parties, and the main teachers' professional associations—the [[New Zealand Educational Institute|NZEI]] and the [[Post Primary Teachers' Association|PPTA]]. Most of the opposition was over the idea of unregistered and untrained teachers having direct contact with students, and the lack of accountability through the Ombudsman and the Official Information Act. In April 2012, [[Massey University]] released a report highly critical of the proposed charter school model, claiming there was no international evidence to support claims that charter schools do better and all they would do is increase segregation.<ref>{{cite web |url= http://www.3news.co.nz/Charter-schools-make-things-worse---study/tabid/423/articleID/250288/Default.aspx |archive-url= https://archive.today/20130624204532/http://www.3news.co.nz/Charter-schools-make-things-worse---study/tabid/423/articleID/250288/Default.aspx |url-status= dead |archive-date= 24 June 2013 |title= Charter schools make things worse – study |publisher=[[3 News New Zealand]] |date= 13 April 2012 |accessdate= 22 June 2013 }}</ref>

The Education Amendment Bill to allow charter schools, rebranded as partnership schools or kura hourua, was introduced on 15 October 2012 and passed its first reading three days later 63 votes to 56.<ref name=":0" /> The bill was referred to the Education and Science [[select committee (parliamentary system)|select committee]] for closer scrutiny and public submissions, with the committee reporting back in April 2013 that the bill should proceed with amendments, including a change so that charter schools would be subject to Ombudsman scrutiny on matters relating to suspensions and expulsions (other than the Ombudsman, the only other way to appeal a suspension or expulsion is through the court system).<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |last=Education and Science Committee |date=12 April 2013 |title=Education Amendment Bill as reported from the Education and Science Committee |url=https://www.parliament.nz/resource/en-NZ/50DBSCH_SCR5823_1/e58b97cd93ef1a8962b3e07045fcc151896f328e |access-date=16 November 2022}}</ref> After the report was released, [[United Future]] MP [[Peter Dunne]] announced he was pulling support for charter schools, leaving only the National, Act and Māori parties supporting the bill.<ref>{{cite news| url= http://www.3news.co.nz/Dunne-against-charter-schools/tabid/1607/articleID/294742/Default.aspx| work= 3 News NZ| title= Dunne against charter schools| date= 18 April 2013| access-date= 4 August 2013| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20131203010645/http://www.3news.co.nz/Dunne-against-charter-schools/tabid/1607/articleID/294742/Default.aspx| archive-date= 3 December 2013| url-status= dead}}</ref> The bill passed its third reading on 4 June 2013, 62 votes to 57.<ref name=":0" />

During the select committee stage, the Labour Party announced that if elected to government at the [[2014 New Zealand general election|2014 general election]], it would introduce provisions to require charter schools to employ registered teachers and make the schools fully accountable to the Ombudsmen and the Official Information Act. It would also not allow any new charter schools and would not guarantee future funding or integration to any existing schools, leaving them either to continue as private schools or close altogether.<ref name=":1" /> While Labour did not win the 2014 election, four Labour MPs were successful in having their [[Private member's bill|member's bills]] related to charter schools selected for introduction between 2015 and 2017. [[Phil Goff]]'s 2015 bill would have required charter schools to teach a "broad and balanced" curriculum;<ref>{{Cite web |title=Education (Charter Schools Curriculum) Amendment Bill - New Zealand Parliament |url=https://www.parliament.nz/en/pb/bills-and-laws/bills-proposed-laws/document/00DBHOH_BILL64528_1/education-charter-schools-curriculum-amendment-bill |access-date=2022-11-16 |website=www.parliament.nz |language=en}}</ref> [[Chris Hipkins]]' 2016 bill proposed their abolition;<ref>{{Cite web |title=Education (Charter Schools Abolition) Amendment Bill - New Zealand Parliament |url=https://www.parliament.nz/en/pb/bills-and-laws/bills-proposed-laws/document/00DBHOH_BILL68842_1/education-charter-schools-abolition-amendment-bill |access-date=2022-11-16 |website=www.parliament.nz |language=en}}</ref> [[Nanaia Mahuta]]'s 2016 bill would have applied the Ombudsmen Act 1975 and [[Official Information Act 1982]] to the charter school regime;<ref name="parliament.nz">{{Cite web |title=Charter Schools (Application of Official Information and Ombudsmen Acts) Bill - New Zealand Parliament |url=https://www.parliament.nz/en/pb/bills-and-laws/bills-proposed-laws/document/00DBHOH_BILL71675_1/charter-schools-application-of-official-information-and |access-date=2022-11-16 |website=www.parliament.nz |language=en}}</ref> [[David Clark (New Zealand politician)|David Clark]]'s 2017 bill would have outlawed for-profit schools.<ref name="parliament.nz"/><ref>{{Cite web |date=16 May 2017 |title=Charter school bill would stop profits being ‘skimmed off’ students’ learning |url=https://www.lawsociety.org.nz/news/newsroom/charter-school-bill-would-stop-profits-being-skimmed-off-students-learning/ |access-date=2026-09-14 |website=New Zealand Law Society {{!}} Te Kāhui Ture o Aotearoa |language=en}}</ref> The first three bills were defeated; the fourth was withdrawn after the change of government in 2017.

== Creation and operations ==
In September 2012, Minister of Education [[Hekia Parata]] announced that many schools in Christchurch would be closed or amalgamated, largely due to population changes and damaged facilities following the [[2011 Christchurch earthquake|February 2011 earthquake]]. However, parents, teachers and students protested at the changes for going too far, and feared that the closures were being used as an excuse to start charter schools. In June 2013, the closed school sites were put up for sale, with charter schools and property development among the proposed uses.<ref>{{cite news|author=McDonald, Liz |url=http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/education/8748364/Shuttered-Chch-schools-to-be-in-demand |title=Shuttered Chch schools to be in demand |date=3 June 2013 |work=stuff.co.nz}}</ref>

Ahead of the passage of the Education Amendment Bill, an independent Partnership Schools Authorisation Board was established in March 2013 to evaluate applications for Partnership Schools and make recommendations to the Minister of Education on which applications should be considered for contract negotiations.<ref name=":2">{{Cite web |title=Partnership Schools Authorisation Board Established |url=https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/partnership-schools-authorisation-board-established |access-date=2022-11-16 |website=The Beehive |language=en}}</ref> [[Catherine Isaac]], a former ACT Party president and candidate, was appointed as chair. The other initial members were former [[PricewaterhouseCoopers|PriceWaterhouseCoopers]] chair John Shewan, Māori language advocate Dame [[Iritana Tāwhiwhirangi|Iritana Tawhiwhirangi]], former [[Auckland Grammar School|Auckland Grammar]] headmaster [[John Morris (New Zealand footballer)|John Morris]], Margaret Southwick, [[Tahu Potiki]] and Terry Bates.<ref name=":2" /> The Board was also responsible for monitoring the educational performance and achievement progress of the Partnership Schools.<ref>{{cite web |title=About Us |url=http://partnershipschools.education.govt.nz/about-us/ |accessdate=8 March 2018 |website=Partnership Schools/Kura Hourua Authorisation Board}}</ref>

In May 2013, the PPTA published in its staff newsletter a list of 21 parties it believed were interested in the charter school programme, which included a high proportion of religious groups.<ref>{{cite news |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/education/8698640/PPTA-outs-charter-school-hopefuls |title=PPTA outs charter school hopefuls |author= Shadwell, Talia |date=21 May 2013 |work= stuff.co.nz}}</ref> While the Ministry of Education refused to release the full list of 36 interested parties, in July 2013, the Ombudsman overruled the Ministry and forced it to release the list as soon as possible.<ref>{{cite news |url= http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=10903240 |title= Ministry ordered to release names of charter school applicants |first= Kate |last= Shuttleworth |publisher=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date= 25 July 2013 |accessdate= 26 July 2013}}</ref> In September 2013, Parata and Banks announced that the first five charter schools would open in [[Auckland]] and [[Northland Region|Northland]] by the first term of 2014. These schools included the [[Vanguard Military School]], the Māori secondary schools Te Kura Hourua o Whangarei Terenga Paraoa and Te Kura Hourua ki Whangaruru, The Rise UP Academy, and the Christian-oriented South Auckland Middle School.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Davison|first1=Isaac|title=Five charter schools to open in 2014|url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=11125874|accessdate=8 March 2018|work=[[The New Zealand Herald]]|date=17 September 2013}}</ref>

In September 2014, the Northland-based Te Kura Hourua ki Whangaruru in [[Whangaruru]] attracted controversy over its poor leadership, high absenteeism of 20%, and mismanagement of government funds. The school, which received 500% more funding than a state school, spent half its income buying a farm. The Ministry of Education carried out a secret inquiry and immediately installed its own manager. One of the two original managers left hurriedly. Problems first arose in 2013 when it was claimed that the school had been set up in a paddock using portaloos for toilets. It was reported that drugs were a problem in the school and that some students had been removed to an unknown place. The school has only one teacher with a current practicing certificate. The original management was replaced by an executive manager from [[Child, Youth and Family]]. The school received $27,000 per student compared to $6,000 per student in a state school.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.ppta.org.nz/communities/president-page/3083-charter-school-quango |title=PPTA News.Vol 35.p3. August 2014. A.Roberts |access-date=8 September 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141006124534/http://www.ppta.org.nz/communities/president-page/3083-charter-school-quango |archive-date=6 October 2014 |url-status=usurped }}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.radionz.co.nz/news/national/254084/charter-school-a-mess-principals|title=Charter school a mess – principals|first=Lois|last=Williams|publisher=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=9 September 2014}}</ref> In October 2015, the Ministry acknowledged that charter schools had been over-funded an extra $888,000 more than they would have been allocated had their funding been strictly based on their enrolments.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Gerritsen|first1=John|title=Ministry says charter schools "over-funding" is $888,000|url=https://www.radionz.co.nz/news/national/287659/charter-schools-'over-funded'-by-$888,000|accessdate=8 March 2018|publisher=[[Radio New Zealand]]|date=22 October 2015}}</ref>

In May 2016, an independent partnership school support entity called E Tipu E Rea was established to provide support for existing and prospective charter school sponsors through a $500,000 conditional grant from the government. The new ACT leader [[David Seymour]], who was appointed parliamentary undersecretary to the Minister of Education after the 2014 election and had been given responsibility for charter schools, claimed that this would bring NZ charter schools in line with overseas models such as England's [[New Schools Network]] and New York City's [[New York Charter School Centre]]. The Board was chaired by former EY Australasia CEO Rob McLeod. Other board members included former [[Māori Party]] co-leader Dame [[Tariana Turia]], Dame Jenny Gibbs, former [[New Zealand national rugby union team|All Black]] [[Michael Jones (rugby union)|La’auli Savae Michael Jones]], Bruce Ritchie, and Kep Rapson.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.voxy.co.nz/politics/5/252150|title=Independent entity to support Partnership Schools|website=Voxy.co.nz|date=20 May 2016|access-date=2 May 2017}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.education.govt.nz/ministry-of-education/information-releases/partnership-schools-kura-hourua-information-release/partnership-schools-policy-development-process/|title=Partnership Schools Policy Development|website=Education in New Zealand|date=4 December 2014 |language=en-NZ|access-date=20 May 2016}}</ref> In August 2016, Seymour announced that two new Māori-oriented charter schools would be opening in Hamilton and Napier by 2017, adding to the eight that were already operating.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Young|first1=Audrey|title=Two new charter schools approved for opening in 2017 in Hamilton and Napier|url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=11701952|accessdate=8 March 2018|work=[[The New Zealand Herald]]|date=30 August 2016}}</ref>

In May 2017, the consultancy firm Martin Jenkins published an independent report on behalf of the Ministry of Education praising most of the country's eight charter schools for helping Māori, Pacific Islander, and disadvantaged children.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Gerritsen|first1=John|title=NZ's charter schools given good report card|url=https://www.radionz.co.nz/news/national/330165/nz's-charter-schools-given-good-report-card|accessdate=8 March 2018|publisher=[[Radio New Zealand]]|date=5 May 2017}}</ref> During the [[2017 New Zealand general election|2017 general election]], the opposition Labour, Green, and [[New Zealand First]] parties announced that they would introduce legislation abolishing charter schools if elected into government.<ref name="Interest Election 2017" /> On 7 September, the National Government announced the creation of four new charter schools including a Christchurch outpost of the Auckland-based [[Vanguard Military School]], a Māori-oriented junior high school, a bilingual Māori high school, and a [[Science, technology, engineering, and mathematics|STEM]]-oriented Auckland school.<ref>{{cite news|title=Four new charter schools announced by govt|url=https://www.radionz.co.nz/news/national/338911/four-new-charter-schools-announced-by-govt|accessdate=8 March 2018|publisher=[[Radio New Zealand]]|date=7 September 2017}}</ref>

